--- title
# Eviz — Business Identity and CRM SaaS

--- body
Digital profiles, content publishing and relationship management.

--- actions
[Back to projects](/en/#products) · [Português](/projects/pt/eviz/)

:::

::: section architecture
## Architecture overview

Eviz combines digital professional profiles, published landing pages, lead acquisition and customer relationship workflows in a SaaS product.

Its major responsibilities are:
- identity and account management;
- editing and publishing digital pages and cards;
- forms, lead intake and relationship records;
- commercial workflows and external services;
- analytics and administrative capabilities.

The public site does not document private tenancy boundaries, internal schemas or integration credentials.
:::

::: section engineering
## Decisions and trade-offs

The platform links the publishing experience with the relationship-management process, reducing handoffs between separate systems. A configurable presentation model supports different page types while sharing a common application layer.

The trade-off is greater coupling among publishing, customer data and commercial workflows. Separating domain responsibilities and treating public-read experiences differently from authenticated editing improves conceptual clarity.
:::

::: section alternatives
## Alternative architectures

A hosted CMS plus third-party CRM would reduce custom development but introduce workflow fragmentation and integration dependency. Separate microservices could isolate scaling and delivery, at the cost of distributed-state and operational complexity. A modular application backend is a reasonable middle ground where domain boundaries are explicit and measured.
:::

::: section verification
## Quality strategy and evidence

The private backend contains unit tests for selected core modules. Available end-to-end coverage is limited in the inspected sources; therefore this page does not claim complete verification of payment, publishing or CRM workflows.

The validation agenda covers account isolation, publication lifecycle, form capture, record consistency, integration errors, accessibility and privacy requirements. No customer volume, conversion or production performance is asserted without audited data.
:::

::: section constraints
## Constraints and evolution

Content publishing, user permissions, transactions and privacy obligations have different failure and regulatory profiles. Evolving the system requires contract tests between domains, stronger end-to-end coverage and observable operational behavior, without exposing internal safeguards publicly.
:::

